{
 "title": "The GT-Score: A Robust Objective Function for Reducing Overfitting in Data-Driven Trading Strategies",
 "hypothesis": "Embedding performance, statistical significance (z-score), consistency (r2) and downside risk (sigma_d) into the optimization objective (GT-Score) reduces overfitting and improves out-of-sample generalization vs Simple (negative profit), Sharpe and Sortino loss functions.",
 "data_sources": [
  "yFinance API daily OHLCV (Open, High, Low, Close, Volume) for top 50 S&P 500 companies by market capitalization"
 ],
 "sample": "Top 50 S&P 500 companies by market capitalization via yFinance API. Daily OHLCV bars. Filters: none beyond top-50 market cap selection; no explicit survivorship, delisting or corporate-action handling stated. Implemented in Python 3.10 custom backtesting engine. Three technical strategies tested on same data: RSI (overbought/oversold thresholds), MACD (fast, slow, signal periods), Bollinger Bands (lookback window, band width). Parameter search spaces: not stated. Return definition for mu, sigma, sigma_d, r2: per-observation strategy vs buy-and-hold benchmark; exact compounding/frequency not stated beyond trade-level N.",
 "period_start": "January 2010",
 "period_end": "December 2024",
 "method": [
  "1. Retrieve daily OHLCV for 50 assets January 2010 through December 2024, approximately 3770 trading days per asset, covering post-2008 recovery, 2020 COVID crash, 2022 rate environment.",
  "2. Define GT-Score composite: GT_Score = mu * ln(z) * r2 / sigma_d, where mu=mean strategy return per observation, mu_m=mean benchmark (buy-and-hold) per observation, sigma=std of strategy returns, N=number of return observations used (trade-level returns in this study, so N=number of executed trades), z=(mu - mu_m)/(sigma / sqrt(N)), ln(z)=natural log significance gate, r2=R-squared consistency, sigma_d=downside deviation. Epsilon=1e-6 added to denominator to prevent division by zero when sigma_d=0.",
  "3. Apply piecewise definition Algorithm 1: if z <=0 score=100+100*(1-exp(-|z-1|)) large penalty; else if z <=1 score=100*(1-exp(-|z-1|)) smooth transition; else score= -(mu*ln(z)*r2)/(sigma_d+epsilon). Impose minimum-trade threshold n_min=50 trades else large penalty. Fixed-threshold mode used for reported results; dissertation stabilized-variance option (search n* where variance of period returns changes <1% threshold, fallback n=50) not used in reported tables but code supports it.",
  "4. Baseline objectives for comparison: Simple Loss = -Profit, Sharpe Ratio Loss = -Sharpe, Sortino Ratio Loss = -Sortino. All objectives allocated same evaluation budget.",
  "5. Walk-forward validation: Training window 4 years, Validation window 2 years, Step size 1 year, Embargo period 30 days between train and validation to prevent leakage. Generates nine sequential train/validation splits per asset covering validation periods 2014-2024. Total 5340 sequential optimization trials reported.",
  "6. Monte Carlo robustness study: Random search optimizer with 25 parameter evaluations per trial. Each configuration run 15 times with different random seeds 42-56. Total 9000 optimization trials (50 assets * 4 loss functions * 3 strategies *15 seeds =2250 per objective).",
  "7. Evaluate out-of-sample: Test Mean, Test Std, Train Mean, Generalization Ratio = Test Return / Training Return (walk-forward: Validation Return / Training Return). Higher ratio indicates better retention.",
  "8. Statistical comparison on Monte Carlo test returns: Paired t-tests (paired across trials) GT-Score minus baseline, reporting Mean Diff, t-Stat, p-Value, Cohen's d; Wilcoxon signed-rank paired non-parametric p-values reported (test statistic not stated). Effect sizes interpreted as small if d<0.2. Note: not data-snooping/selection-bias corrected; treated as descriptive.",
  "9. Regime and strategy breakdowns: validation returns by 9 walk-forward periods (2-year windows) vs Baseline Avg (mean across Sharpe, Sortino, Simple) and Delta in percentage points (GT minus Baseline Avg); mean OOS return by strategy (RSI, MACD, Bollinger).",
  "10. Transaction-cost sensitivity: lightweight analysis on Monte Carlo OOS returns subtracting additional per-side cost proportional to number of test-window trades, tested range 0-10 bps per trade side (entry and exit). Mean test-window trades reported 32.4 for GT-Score vs ~21.0 for baselines. No instrument-specific execution modeling (commissions, spreads, slippage, liquidity) in main tables."
 ],
